File.write("#{d}/NOTE_SUMMARY.md",<<~MD)
# Research-note summary

The present ATLAS snapshot contains formal constructions with exact order and simplicity proofs for three families—prime cyclic groups, alternating groups of degree at least five, and projective special linear groups over arbitrary finite fields with the two standard small exceptions—and fifteen sporadic groups. These are existence/construction results, not a formal proof of the classification of finite simple groups.

The sporadic constructions comprise the five Mathieu groups, the three simple Conway groups, McLaughlin, Higman–Sims, Suzuki, Janko J2, and the three Fischer simple groups. Their carriers are built from Golay designs, the Leech lattice and its stabilizers/scalar structures, or the Conway–Parker algebra and its reflecting-ray geometry. Central covers and full nonsimple symmetry groups are kept separate. No VOA or Moonshine construction is inferred from the Fischer algebra.

#{table}

The current build passes 5,203 targets. Fresh compiled extraction covers #{t['selected']} selected declarations and a transitive graph of #{t['nodes']} declarations. The only axiom constants encountered are propositional extensionality, classical choice, and quotient soundness. The audit retains full hypotheses and group definitions, since an axiom list alone cannot establish the intended mathematical meaning of a theorem.

Tracked mathematical source comprises #{core} physical lines excluding designated root/audit/smoke-check drivers. The global deduplicated ATLAS source footprint of the 36 selected order/simplicity proofs is #{c['principal_atlas_union']} lines. Shared dependencies prevent summing per-group totals. Generated-data provenance is not reliably separable; external source footprints are incomplete where compiler ranges are absent.

Further goals include eleven remaining sporadics, the remaining families, and stronger classification-facing recognition interfaces. Existing unimported drafts are not promoted to completed results. The computational and logical assurance is conditional on Lean's foundations and correct kernel implementation; neither this audit nor its axiom reports establish metatheoretic consistency or hardware correctness. See STATUS.md, FOUNDATIONS.md, DEPENDENCY_OVERVIEW.md, and the exact machine-readable evidence before quoting narrower independence or verification claims.
